  "notes": [
    "Evaluation redefined per Rudiger's direction: the comparison is CCPM-with-skill vs a deterministic traditional-CPM baseline (ASAP, safe durations, resources ignored, no buffers) - not with-skill vs without-skill. Configurations: with_skill = CCPM, cpm_baseline = CPM reference.",
    "Headline result: all 4 CCPM schedules are fully resource-leveled (0 overloaded resource-days) with one project buffer and feeding buffers attached via :PB/:FB links, and all passed the skill's validator. The CPM baselines overload resources in 3 of 4 projects (website 2, kitchen 8, retrofit 4 overloaded resource-days) - visible as red blocks in each comparison.png.",
    "Lab-trials is the honest negative: its ASAP CPM layout happens to be conflict-free, so the 'CPM shows overloading' assertion fails there. Not every network overloads under CPM; the skill's value there is the buffer structure, not leveling.",
    "Promise-date story: CPM promises the bare critical-path end (e.g. retrofit day 36 with an infeasible plan); CCPM finishes the work earlier on aggressive durations (day 23) and promises day 35 with 12 days of explicit protection - a slightly earlier AND feasible AND protected commitment.",
    "Noted for a future skill iteration: the deterministic earlier-only leveling rule can be suboptimal (retrofit subagent reported a ~20-day hand-optimized chain vs 23 produced). Determinism was chosen deliberately for CLI convertibility; revisit if schedule quality matters more than reproducibility."
  ]
